  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Just a few showers left along the line. Some \npatchy fog is possible overnight. \n\nThe few showers left out there continue to dissipate. \n\nThe cold front and much drier air are starting to get into PA,\nnow. Expect that drier air to continue sliding across PA\novernight. By morning, the northern mtns may have dewpoints in \nthe upper 40s, and even the far srn tier will have 55-60F Tds. \nLittle fog is expected as the dry airmass moves in - and some \nclouds, too, just behind the front. But, some of the northern \nvalleys will probably have a little fog as they temps there\nshould become colder than the river/creek water temps.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A taste of fall to start the workweek will be \nfollowed by increasing humidity and chances for rain for the \nsecond half of the week.\n\nDewpoints will fall rapidly behind the cold front, from near 70\nthis afternoon to the 40s and 50s by early Monday. The northern\ntier will see some low stratus downwind of the Great Lakes on \nMonday in NW flow. Otherwise, expect clear skies and a pleasant \nSeptember afternoon across much of central PA on Monday. With \nhigh pressure building overhead, radiational cooling will be \neffective Monday night bringing temperatures down into the 40s \nfor much of central PA with patchy river valley fog possible.\nValley fog will be possible again Tuesday night.\n\nReturn flow on the back side of the high pressure system moving\noffshore will bring increasing humidity for the middle and\nlatter part of the week. A slight chance for precip returns to\nthe forecast by mid week, with increasing chances for rain\nby early in the following week as the upper level pattern\nbecomes more amplified.",
